Contract format: The schema document is a YAML/JSON mapping. To be format-tolerant and useful for F1/F2 drafts, I'll define a canonical section layout but accept aliases:

```yaml
class_id: AF-WCC-VAC-GEN
title: ...
quantifiers:
  forall: ...
  exists: ...
topology:
  dimension: 4
  symmetry: spherically symmetric
  asymptotics: asymptotically flat
  manifold: ...
data_class:
  regularity: C^2
  weighted_norm: ...
  decay: ...
matter: vacuum        # or scalar with potential
genericity:
  kind: open_dense     # full_measure | baire
  parameter_space: ...
i_plus:
  name: future null infinity (I+)
  visibility: complete
conclusion:
  type: theorem
  statement: ...
  class_object: ...
```

Check implementations (deterministic, each check returns (ok, code, detail)):

- load doc; if not mapping → reject `DOC_SHAPE`.
- `CLASS_ID`: exactly one of allowed; if `class_id` missing → reject. If doc contains a list/string with more than one class token anywhere in class_id field → leak.
- `CONJUNCTION`: search conclusion.statement + conclusion.type + any text for regex `\b(or|either)\b` near class tokens C0/C2, or literally "C0 or C2", "C0/C2", "C^0 or C^2", "C2/C0". Flag.
- `QUANTIFIERS`: require `quantifiers` mapping with keys containing forall/exists (aliases: ∀, exists, there exists) and non-empty values; vagueness lexicon scan across whole doc: ["some suitable", "appropriate", "reasonable", "suitable", "sufficiently generic", "etc.", "and so on", "maybe", "possibly", "roughly", "as needed", "if necessary"]. Careful: "suitable" might legitimately appear... but the point of F1 is exact quantifiers, so flag it. Also require quantifier text to contain "for all"/"for every"/"∀" and "there exists"/"∃".
- `TOPOLOGY`: require `topology` mapping with keys dimension (int or parseable) and at least one of symmetry/asymptotics/manifold; dimension must be 4 for these classes; note spherical symmetry for the -SPH classes.
- `DATA_CLASS`: require `data_class` with `regularity` matching regex `C\^?\s*\d|C\^?\\infty|H\^?\d|W\^\{?\d|Sobolev|BV|L\^p|smooth|analytic`; and a weight/decay key with a norm/weight token.
- `MATTER`: if class_id contains "VAC" require matter ∈ {vacuum, T=0, Ricci-flat} and regularity; if "SCALAR" require matter mentions scalar and potential. Also cross-check: VAC doc must not mention scalar field in matter.
- `GENERICITY`: require `genericity` with kind ∈ {open_dense, full_measure, baire, residual} and non-empty `parameter_space`; reject bare "generic" if parameter space missing. Also scan for "for generic initial data" without parameter space.
- `I_PLUS`: require `i_plus` (aliases: iplus, scri_plus, future_null_infinity) with visibility ∈ {complete, future_complete, visible, trapped, censored...}. For WCC classes, require explicit I+ visibility statement. For SCC classes, I+ visibility isn't the defining property; require either i_plus or a "no escape to I+"? Hmm. Per hard decision: "AF-WCC vacuum schema with exact quantifiers, topology, weighted data class, genericity, I+, visibility, and conclusion type" — that's F1 for WCC. For F2 SCC schemas, the ASTRA queue says "write separate C2 and C0 strong-censorship schemas". I'll make I_PLUS required for AF-WCC-* classes, and for AF-SCC-* require `extension`/`inextendibility` section instead. Let me define check 9 more carefully:
  - For class ids starting with AF-WCC: require `i_plus` with `visibility` and require the conclusion statement to reference "visible" / "naked singularity" / "I+"? Require presence.
  - For AF-SCC: require `inextendibility` section with `regularity` ∈ {C0, C2} matching the class id (C0 → C^0, C2 → C^2), and `extension` mode ("no extension", "inextendible"); require conclusion to be about inextendibility/extension, not about visibility to I+.
- `CONCLUSION_TYPE`: conclusion.type ∈ allowed set; for WCC require theorem/conditional_theorem? Actually allow any except numerical_evidence/formal_model? Keep allowed set same as schema; but require conclusion.statement non-empty and `class_object` present.
- `SCOPE_SEPARATION` (leakage): 
  - WCC doc must not contain SCC-only tokens in conclusion: "inextendible", "inextendibility" (unless in a comparison section explicitly marked `scope_note`? To keep it deterministic and simple: scan conclusion.statement only, plus conclusion.type. That reduces false positives.)
